      "polar of a point ON the circle is the tangent there:\n   P =  [1,0,1]    polar =  [1,0,-1]    P on its own polar?  true\n   P =  [0,1,1]    polar =  [0,1,-1]    P on its own polar?  true\n   P =  [3,4,5]    polar =  [3,4,-5]    P on its own polar?  true\nconjugacy is symmetric:  Pᵀ S Q = Qᵀ S P\n   P on polar(Q)?  9      Q on polar(P)?  9\nsix points on the circle:\n                          [[1,0,1],[0,1,1],[-(3/5),4/5,1],[-(4/5),3/5,1],\n                           [0,-1,1],[-(3/5),-(4/5),1]]\nall on the circle?  [true,true,true,true,true,true]\nAB ∩ DE =  [-2,3,1]\nBC ∩ EF =  [-3,0,1]\nCD ∩ FA =  [-(19/5),-(12/5),1]\ndet =  0    ⟹ collinear: the Pascal line\nPascal line =  [1/3,-(1/9),1]\ncircumscribed hexagon vertices:\n                                [[1,1,1],[-(1/3),1,1],[-(5/7),5/7,1],\n                                 [-2,-1,1],[-(1/3),-1,1],[1,-2,1]]\nits three main diagonals meet? det =  0\n   concurrent at  [-(1/3),1/9,1]   — Brianchon's theorem"
     ]
