   "source": "## Summary\n\n| Fact | Form |\n|---|---|\n| a conic is a symmetric matrix | $x^{\\mathsf T} S x = 0$, $S$ up to scale |\n| conics form a 5-dimensional family | $6 - 1 = 5$ |\n| projectivities act by congruence | $S \\mapsto M^{-\\mathsf T} S M^{-1}$ |\n| the only invariant is signature | Sylvester's law of inertia |\n| **one non-degenerate real conic** | all have signature $(2,1)$ up to sign |\n| five points determine a conic | five linear conditions on $S$ |\n| polar of $P$ | the line $SP$; the tangent when $P \\in C$ |\n| Pascal / Brianchon | dual, related by polarity in the conic |\n\nThe headline result deserves restating. In 1.06 the eigenvalues of $A$ sorted conics into three species; here congruence scrambles eigenvalues and preserves only their **signs**, and all three species have the same signs. Ellipse, parabola and hyperbola are one curve, distinguished purely by their relationship to a line we singled out and called infinity — and we exhibited the projectivity $[x:y:z] \\mapsto [z:y:x]$ that converts a circle into a hyperbola by swapping that line with the $y$-axis.\n\nThe final computation is the one to remember: the **Brianchon point is the pole of the Pascal line**. Two theorems that look unrelated in Euclidean geometry are a single theorem viewed through polarity — the duality of 2.02 upgraded to respect a conic.\n\n## What Stage 2 has established so far\n\nWe have now removed, in order: the metric (1.05), the line at infinity (2.02), any preferred 4 points (2.03), and any distinction between conic types (2.04). Almost nothing is left — and that is the point. Projective geometry is the floor of the Erlangen ladder, the geometry with the largest group and the fewest invariants.\n\nThe remaining notebooks build *back up*, by adding structure of a different kind. Rather than restoring the Euclidean metric, they add a **conic** and measure with it. Fixing a conic and keeping only the projectivities that preserve it gives a subgroup — and that subgroup turns out to be an isometry group:\n\n| Fixed conic | Subgroup preserving it | Geometry |\n|---|---|---|\n| a real conic, points inside | $PSL(2,\\mathbb R)$ | **hyperbolic** (2.06) |\n| an imaginary conic | $O(3)$ | **elliptic / spherical** (2.07) |\n| a degenerate conic (double line) | affine, then Euclidean | back to Stage 1 |\n\nThis is the **Cayley–Klein** construction, and it is the reason 2.03 spent so long on the cross-ratio: hyperbolic distance will be defined *as* a logarithm of a cross-ratio taken against the fixed conic. Distance, having been demolished, is about to be rebuilt out of purely projective materials.\n\n## Next\n\n**2.05 — Inversive Geometry and Möbius Maps** takes the complex route to the same place: circles and lines become a single kind of object on the Riemann sphere, and $PSL(2,\\mathbb C)$ acts on them.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. Chapter 6 for projective conics and Pascal.\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. §14 for conics over a field; the Cayley–Klein use of a fixed conic underlies its Chapter 7.\n- Berger, M. (1987) *Geometry II*. Berlin: Springer. Chapters 14–16 give the full projective classification of quadrics, of which the conic case is done here.\n- Richter-Gebert, J. (2011) *Perspectives on Projective Geometry*. Berlin: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-642-17286-1. Conics, polarity and the Cayley–Klein construction, developed with the same matrix calculus used in this notebook.\n- Samuel, P. (1988) *Projective Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. ISBN 978-0-387-96752-3. Chapter 4 on conics and quadrics.\n\nPascal proved his theorem in 1640 at the age of sixteen; Brianchon published the dual in 1806, before duality was understood as a general principle.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
